Sprint number: Sprint 2
File reviewed: Enemy.cs
Author of file reviewed: Colin
 
COMMENTS ON CODE QUALITY:
- Dual-field design (enemySpriteAnim / enemySprite): having two mutually-exclusive
  nullable fields, with every Update/Draw call branching on which one is null, is a
  recurring source of bugs as the class grows - it's easy to add a new code path
  later and forget to handle both cases (Draw already shows the asymmetry: one
  branch uses a plain null check, the other uses null-conditional ?.). A cleaner
  approach would be a single ISprite-typed field (ISprite already exists in the
  project) so Update/Draw don't need to know which concrete sprite type is in use.
- RandomGenerator is already static readonly, which is good practice and is the
  right model to follow for the directions array as well.
 
HYPOTHETICAL CHANGE:
Suppose a future sprint adds an enemy type that chases Link instead of wandering
randomly (e.g. a more aggressive enemy). The current implementation would only
partially support this:
- GetNextMovement is virtual, so a subclass could override it to return a direction
  toward Link - that part of the design is genuinely extensible and a good sign.
- However, Enemy has no reference to Link's position anywhere in the class (no
  field, no parameter passed into the constructor or into Update/GetNextMovement).
  A chase-enemy subclass would have nothing to chase with the current API - Link's
  position would need to be threaded in, which means changing the constructor
  signature or the Update/GetNextMovement signature, which in turn means touching
  every existing call site and every existing subclass, not just adding a new one.
In short: the virtual-method hook makes new movement behavior possible in theory,
but the missing player-position data is a real gap that would force changes beyond
just adding a new subclass.
